OBSERVATION (not a criterion; for the lead, no action implied by me)
  I recovered the adversarial probe and analysed the edge it pokes at. An
  occurrence whose UTC calendar-day LABEL equals asOf's LOCAL day label, but
  whose instant is after asOf's instant (e.g. asOf 2026-09-01 08:00 JST =
  2026-08-31 23:00Z, NextExpected 2026-09-01 00:00Z), is written to key
  {2026,Sep,1} but never read, because the balance walk covers labels
  asOfDay+1..+35. I reproduced this myself with a yearly item
  (<scratch>/r12indep/.../zz_checker_yearly_test.go): Crossing zero,
  Minimum 800.00, occurrence not applied.
  My analysis: this is NOT a regression and NOT a residual money defect.
  (i) Pre-fix the same input drops everything, so R12 is strictly better.
  (ii) The outcome is what a fully label-consistent implementation would also
  produce: a label-based advance loop would step that occurrence to
  today+interval, which for the yearly case is outside the 35-day horizon and
  for the monthly case is 2026-10-01 - exactly the dates the current code
  already yields (I ran the monthly variant: Crossing=2026-10-01,
  Minimum=400.00, PASS).
  (iii) The only inconsistency left is internal: the advance loop compares
  instants (`!occ.After(asOfDay)`, projection.go:158) while the read window
  compares labels. The extra write it produces is always at a label <= asOf's
  label, and labels <= asOf are never read, so the inconsistency is
  unobservable in Project's output. The brief explicitly forbade changing the
  instant comparisons shared with BalanceAt et al.
  The probe's expectation (that a sub-day-future occurrence should count today)
  is an instant-based reading of the semantics; the codebase's documented
  convention is calendar-day-in-its-own-location, under which the current
  behaviour is correct. I record it so the lead can rule if a second checker
  raises it.

